---
title: Text Transforms
jupyter: python3
html-table-processing: none
guide-section: Formatting Data
bread-crumbs: false
---

Sometimes the final step in preparing a table involves modifying the text content of cells after all
formatting has been applied. The `text_*()` methods operate on the already-rendered text in cells,
giving you a post-processing layer for tasks like replacing abbreviations, applying conditional
labels, or inserting custom HTML. These methods complement the `fmt_*()` methods, which work on raw
data values.

To clarify the distinction: `fmt_*()` methods work on raw data values *before* they become text.
Text transforms work on the *already-rendered text* after formatting. This means you can use
`fmt_number()` to get consistent decimal places, and then use `text_transform()` to wrap the result
in custom HTML or append a unit label. The two layers complement each other, where formatting
handles the numeric conversion, and text transforms handle the final presentation touches.

## Setting Up the Example Data

::: {#17cfa9cb .cell execution_count=1}
``` {.python .cell-code}
import polars as pl
from great_tables import GT, loc, md

status_df = pl.DataFrame({
    "task": ["Data collection", "Analysis", "Report writing", "Peer review"],
    "status": ["DONE", "IN_PROGRESS", "NOT_STARTED", "DONE"],
    "priority": ["high", "high", "medium", "low"],
    "progress": [100, 65, 0, 100],
})

gt_tbl = GT(status_df, rowname_col="task")
gt_tbl
```

