This approach is particularly powerful when your replacement logic depends on the value itself (such
as numeric thresholds) rather than exact string matching.

The key difference from `text_case_match()` is that `text_case_when()` is predicate-based: each case
is a function that can express numeric thresholds, string containment tests, length checks, or any
other boolean logic. Use it when your replacement logic goes beyond exact string matching. If you
find yourself writing predicates that are just `lambda x: x == "some_value"`, that's a sign you
should use `text_case_match()` instead.

## Targeting Different Locations

All text methods support the `locations=` argument, which defaults to `loc.body()` when not
specified. You can target other parts of the table as well.

### Transforming Row Group Labels

::: {#352a31c6 .cell execution_count=10}
``` {.python .cell-code}
df_grouped = pl.DataFrame({
    "name": ["Alice", "Bob", "Charlie", "Diana"],
    "team": ["team_alpha", "team_alpha", "team_beta", "team_beta"],
    "score": [92, 87, 95, 78],
})

(
    GT(df_grouped, rowname_col="name", groupname_col="team")
    .text_replace(
        pattern=r"team_(\w+)",
        replacement=r"Team \1",
        locations=loc.row_groups()
    )
)
```

